Stars much heavier than the Sun burn fast and [[@star-life-cycle|die young]]. When the fuel at the centre of such a star runs out, the core collapses in under a second. The outer layers bounce off and blast into space as a [[@supernova|supernova]], which can briefly outshine a whole [[galaxy|galaxy]].

What remains of the core becomes a neutron star. In the year 1054, Chinese [[astronomer|astronomers]] recorded a new star bright enough to see in daylight for weeks. Today, at that spot, sits the Crab Nebula, with a neutron star spinning 30 times a second at its heart.

{{story:black-holes}}

A drone is naturally wobbly. A gust of wind or a tiny difference between motors would flip it over in a second. So the flight controller watches constantly.

It uses gyroscopes, which sense turning, and accelerometers, which sense speeding up and tilting. These are tiny chips, the same kind that let your phone know when you turn it sideways. If the drone starts to tip, the computer speeds up the motors on the low side within thousandths of a second. A [[barometer|barometer]] senses height from air pressure, and [[@gps-how|GPS]] tells the drone where it is on the map.

{{story:robots}}

In 2001, scientists found that the collapsing bubble also makes a flash of light, far too faint and short for human eyes. They nicknamed it "shrimpoluminescence". The colour of the light told them how hot the bubble got for that instant, which is how the estimate of over 4,000 °C was made.

The heat lasts less than a millionth of a second, and the bubble is smaller than a grain of sand. You couldn't cook anything with it. But for a blink, a patch of seawater gets almost as hot as the surface of [[@sun-what|the Sun]].

{{story:sun-what}}

The Romans built with concrete about 2,000 years ago. They mixed lime with volcanic ash from near Naples. Their harbour walls, soaked by the sea for centuries, have grown stronger as seawater reacted with the ash.

The Pantheon in Rome has a concrete dome about 43 metres across, wider than two cricket pitches laid end to end, with a round hole open to the sky at the top. Roman builders used lighter stones near the top so the dome would not crush itself. It was finished around 125 CE and is still in use. Engineers keep studying [[@ancient-rome|ancient Rome's]] recipes for ideas.

{{story:ancient-rome}}

On the International [[@space-station|Space Station]], there's no "up" for hot gases to rise to. NASA has studied this with careful experiments. Without rising air, a candle flame forms a small, dim, round ball, often blue. It burns slowly, because fresh oxygen only drifts in gradually.

These experiments help scientists understand burning better, and they help keep spacecraft safe. A fire in a sealed cabin is one of the most dangerous things that can happen in space. Astronauts can't step outside or open a window, so the station carries special extinguishers and smoke detectors in every module.

{{story:fire-discovery}}

The rule behind this is called Archimedes' principle, after the Greek mathematician from Syracuse. A Roman writer, Vitruvius, told a famous story about him two centuries later. A king wanted to know if his new crown was pure gold or had cheaper silver mixed in. Archimedes, stepping into a full bath, saw water spill over the side. He realised he could measure the crown's [[@area-volume|volume]] by the water it pushed aside. Then, the story says, he ran through the streets shouting "Eureka!", meaning "I have found it!"

Historians doubt the bath story happened like that. But Archimedes did write a book on floating bodies, and the principle carries his name.

{{story:why-sea-salty}}

Iron and some kinds of steel are magnetic, so the field grips them strongly and heats them fast. Aluminium, copper and glass don't work the same way, so on most induction stoves they stay cold. That's why induction pans often have a thick steel disc on the bottom.

There's an easy test, which you can try with any [[@how-magnets-fridge|fridge magnet]]. If a fridge [[@magnets-how|magnet]] sticks firmly to the base, the pan will work. Many Indian kitchens found this out when they bought an induction stove and their old aluminium pressure cooker refused to whistle. Companies now sell "induction-base" cookers and kadhais.

{{story:kitchen-safety}}

A microphone is [[@how-headphones|a headphone running in reverse]]. Sound waves shake its diaphragm, the diaphragm moves a coil past a magnet, and that movement makes a tiny electric current. The current copies the shape of the sound.

That simple pairing makes all recorded sound possible. The microphone turns sound into [[@electricity-made|electricity]]. Something stores it. Later a speaker turns the electricity back into sound, ready for your [[@ears-hear|ears]].

{{story:ears-hear}}

रंग लाख से आता है। लाख एक राल है, जिसे नन्हे कीड़े बनाते हैं जो *पलाश* और *कुसुम* जैसे पेड़ों का रस चूसते हैं। हज़ारों कीड़े एक टहनी पर जमघट लगाकर उस पर एक पपड़ी चढ़ा देते हैं। लोग यह पपड़ी खुरचकर निकालते हैं, साफ़ करते हैं और पिघलाते हैं।

कारीगर पिघली लाख में पौधों से बने [[pigment|रंग]] मिलाते हैं और उसे मोटी क्रेयॉन जैसी बत्तियों में बेल लेते हैं। फिर घूमते खिलौने पर बत्ती दबाते हैं। [[@friction|घर्षण]] से लाख गर्म होकर पिघलती है और बराबर फैल जाती है। आख़िर में वे केवड़े का सूखा पत्ता खिलौने पर दबाकर उसे चमका देते हैं। उन छोटे-छोटे [[@insects-importance|कीड़ों]] के बिना चन्नपटना की चमक नहीं होती।

{{story:toys-history}}

रोशनी और परछाईं के तीखे फ़र्क को कलाकार [[chiaroscuro|कियारोस्कूरो]] कहते हैं, यह इतालवी शब्द है जिसका मतलब है "उजाला-अँधेरा"। रेम्ब्रांट इसके उस्ताद थे। वे अक्सर पृष्ठभूमि गहरी रंगते और फिर रोशनी वाले हिस्सों पर मोटा रंग चढ़ाते जाते, कभी-कभी इतना मोटा कि वह कैनवस से केक पर लगी क्रीम की तरह उभर आता।

पास से देखो तो उनकी कूची का काम खुरदुरा लग सकता है। थोड़ा पीछे हटो, तो वही त्वचा, सुनहरे धागे या आँख की चमक बन जाता है। उनकी एचिंग, जो धातु की प्लेट पर डिज़ाइन खुरचकर और फिर छापकर बनती थी, उनकी पेंटिंग से बहुत पहले पूरे यूरोप में उनका नाम फैला चुकी थी।

{{story:mughals}}

Once people are safe, they need the basics. Dirty water can spread diseases like cholera, so relief teams bring purifying tablets, tankers and pumps. Camps are set up in schools, stadiums or tents. Families who have lost everything also need simple things, like cooking pots, sanitary pads and a phone charger.

Charities and groups like the [[@red-cross|Red Cross]] hand out food, blankets and hygiene kits. Doctors check for injuries and illness. Volunteers help families find relatives who were separated, sometimes using lists on noticeboards and, today, phone apps.

{{story:weather-satellites}}

Over 2,000 years ago, Eratosthenes, a Greek scholar in Egypt, found a simple way to list primes. Write out numbers, then cross out multiples of 2, then of 3, then of 5, and so on. The ones left standing are prime. Computers still use versions of his sieve.

Today, volunteers around the world run a project called the Great Internet Mersenne Prime Search, using spare computer time to hunt for giant primes. In October 2024, it confirmed the largest prime known so far, with more than 41 million digits. It was found by Luke Durant, a former engineer, who used thousands of graphics processors in [[@data-centres|cloud data centres]].

{{story:online-safety}}

Since 2014 the cars have used a 1.6-litre turbocharged V6 engine joined to electric motors. Energy from braking is stored in a battery and released for extra power. From 2014 to 2025, energy from hot exhaust gases was collected too, but new rules in 2026 dropped that part and made the electric motor much stronger. Together they produce roughly 1,000 horsepower.

The [[@brakes|brakes]] are just as dramatic. They can slow a car from over 300 km/h to under 100 km/h in a few seconds. Carbon brake discs glow orange and can reach about 1,000°C.

{{story:friction}}

For centuries, Ganesh Chaturthi was celebrated in homes and temples. In 1893, the freedom fighter [[@tilak|Bal Gangadhar Tilak]] promoted public celebrations in Pune and Bombay. Gathering in the streets let people meet, talk and organise at a time when British rulers restricted political meetings.

The public festival, called Sarvajanik Ganeshotsav, spread quickly. It became part of the story of India's [[@freedom-struggle|freedom struggle]]. Today Maharashtra has tens of thousands of public Ganesh pandals, each run by a local committee.

{{story:freedom-struggle}}

In 1999, aged 16, she played her first one-day international, against Ireland. She scored 114 not out. Three years later, at Taunton in England, she made 214 in a Test, then the highest score in women's [[@test-cricket|Test cricket]].

Mithali was famous for timing rather than power. She leaned into drives and placed the ball into gaps between fielders. She was rarely in a hurry, which made her very hard to get out. Bowlers knew that if they tried to tempt her with wide balls, she would simply leave them and wait. Her ODI batting average was above 50, a mark of great consistency in any kind of cricket. She became the first woman to pass 6,000 ODI runs, and later 7,000.

{{story:cricket-stats}}

Early NBA games could be slow. In 1954 the league added the 24-second shot clock: a team must shoot within 24 seconds of getting the ball. Scoring went up straight away.

In 1979 the NBA added the three-point line. At first teams rarely used it. Today it is central to the game, and some teams take around 40 three-point shots a night. That is partly thanks to Stephen Curry of the Golden State Warriors, who has made more three-pointers than anyone in history.

{{story:basketball}}

When Germany invaded Poland in 1939 and then much of Europe, millions more Jewish people came under Nazi control. Many were forced into crowded, walled-off parts of cities called ghettos, with little food.

From 1941, the Nazis moved to killing on a massive scale. Special units shot huge numbers of people in eastern Europe. The Nazis also built death camps in occupied Poland, and sent people there by train from all over Europe. Auschwitz-Birkenau was the largest. About one million Jewish people were killed there.

{{story:anne-frank}}

The soldiers stand about 1.8 m tall on average, and each is unique in some way. They have different faces, hairstyles, moustaches and expressions. Generals are taller, with fancy headgear. Archers kneel or stand ready to shoot.

How was this possible? Workers used moulds for the main body parts, then added clay by hand for faces, hair and details. Some figures carry names of workshop leaders stamped into the clay, a kind of quality control. Scientists studying the ears of soldiers in 2014 found no two pairs exactly the same, suggesting real people may have modelled for some faces.

{{story:archaeology}}

In 1863, Lincoln's Emancipation Proclamation declared enslaved people in rebel states free. As Union armies advanced, that freedom became real, town by town. Black soldiers fought in growing numbers.

The war ended in 1865, and the 13th Amendment was ratified that December. News travelled slowly. On 19 June 1865, a Union general arrived in Galveston, [[@texas|Texas]], and announced that all enslaved people there were free. Celebrations of that day, called Juneteenth, began the next year.

{{story:human-rights}}

The First World War turned planes from wooden toys into fighting machines. Afterwards, airlines began carrying mail and passengers. In 1932 J.R.D. Tata flew India's first airmail service, from Karachi to Bombay, in a small single-engine plane.

Planes got bigger and were built of aluminium instead of cloth. But [[propeller|propellers]] have a speed limit, and engineers wanted more. In 1939 a German plane, the Heinkel He 178, made the first flight powered by a [[@plane-jet|jet engine]]. In 1947 the American pilot Chuck Yeager flew faster than sound in the rocket-powered Bell X-1.

{{story:rockets-how}}

Around 1450, in the city of Mainz, a goldsmith named [[@printing-revolution|Johannes Gutenberg]] built a printing press with movable metal letters. Each letter could be set, inked, pressed, cleaned and reused. His most famous book, a Bible, was printed by about 1455.

Before this, books in Europe were mostly copied by hand, which could take months. Within fifty years, printers across Europe had produced millions of books. Ideas, news and arguments spread faster than ever before.

{{story:printing-press}}

The Hooghly is wide and busy with boats, so the city needed a bridge that would not block them. [[@howrah-bridge|Howrah Bridge]], opened in 1943, is a cantilever bridge: two huge steel arms reach out from the banks and meet in the middle, with no pillars in the river at all. It is about 705 m long.

The steel is held together by rivets, not nuts and bolts. Today the bridge carries a huge stream of cars, buses, handcarts and people on foot every day.

{{story:bridges}}

For years, Texas was cattle country. Cowboys drove herds of longhorn cattle north to railways in Kansas. Then, in 1901, a well at Spindletop, near Beaumont, struck [[@oil-coal|oil]], which shot more than 40 m into the air. Texas became one of the biggest oil producers in the world. Today it also produces more [[@renewable-energy|wind power]] than any other state.

In Houston is NASA's Johnson Space Center, home of Mission Control. When [[@moon-landing-sites|Apollo 11]] landed on the Moon in 1969, Neil Armstrong radioed "Houston, Tranquility Base here. The Eagle has landed." Astronauts still train in Houston. They practise spacewalks in a giant swimming pool that holds a full-size copy of parts of the International [[@space-station|Space Station]].

{{story:apollo-moon}}

In English, frogs say "ribbit". But most frogs don't sound like that at all. Many people think the word spread through Hollywood films. These often used recordings of one small frog from the US West Coast, the Pacific tree frog, whose call does sound a bit like "ribbit". India alone has more than 400 kinds of frogs and toads, and they croak, whistle, click and peep.

Japanese frogs say "kero kero" or "gero gero". In Hindi, frogs croak "tarr tarr", or "tarr-tarr" in the [[monsoon|monsoon]], when they call from every puddle.

{{story:whale-songs}}

स्वीडन में सुरस्ट्रोमिंग बनाने के लिए बाल्टिक सागर की हेरिंग मछली को पीपों में सड़ने के लिए छोड़ दिया जाता है, फिर डिब्बों में बंद कर दिया जाता है, जहाँ उसका ख़मीर उठता रहता है। गैस से डिब्बे फूल तक जाते हैं। लोग इन्हें आम तौर पर घर के बाहर खोलते हैं, क्योंकि गंध बहुत ही तेज़ होती है।

आइसलैंड में हाकार्ल मिलता है, ग्रीनलैंड शार्क का मांस। ताज़ा हो तो यह ज़हरीला होता है। इसलिए इसे हफ़्तों तक सड़ाया जाता है और फिर महीनों तक झोपड़ियों में टाँगकर सुखाया जाता है, जब तक यह खाने लायक़ न हो जाए। इसकी गंध की तुलना तेज़ सफ़ाई वाले रसायनों से की गई है। दोनों खाने ठंडी जगहों से आते हैं, जहाँ लोगों को सर्दियों के लिए मछली बचाकर रखनी पड़ती थी, और [[@fermentation|ख़मीर उठाने]] की कला ने बिना फ़्रिज के यह मुमकिन किया।

{{story:fermentation}}

A flower has a small swollen part at its centre called the ovary, which holds tiny egg cells. When [[pollen|pollen]] from another flower reaches it, the eggs become seeds and the ovary swells into a fruit.

Some fruits are soft and juicy, like mangoes, which you can read about in [[@mango-tree|the mango tree]]. Others are dry, like a pea pod or a [[@coconut|coconut]]. Each is a different way of moving seeds away from the parent plant. Juicy fruits get eaten by animals, which drop the seeds somewhere else. Dry ones may split, float or catch the wind.

{{story:seeds-fly}}
